{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15375-geist-story-plot-hooks","id":15375,"title":"Geist Story & Plot Hooks","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Geist: The Sin-Eaters"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-21T07:55:59-08:00","id":15375,"title":"Geist Story & Plot Hooks","content":"Hey everyone! I'm relatively new to the world of Geist, but not to World of darkenss. I have just been asked to run a Geist Campaign. ALthough I have read the books (Geist:Core/Book of the Dead) I am really at a loss for story ideas and plot hooks. Can anyone help me with this? Any suggestions would be appreciated.<br /> <br /> Thanks,<br /> <br /> Bigwhitt<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Nothing is foolproof for a sufficiently talented fool.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/981-bigwhitt","name":"bigwhitt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar981_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-21T18:56:22-08:00","id":15781,"title":"","content":"I honestly couldn't think of what to do with a Geist game till I played in a mortal game where we ran around under employment from a Sin Eater (I called him the Don of the Dead Mafia... the table died, so I didn't apologize about the pun). Most of what we did was collecting Mementos from recent deaths (that were, unbeknownst to anyone, involved in some Infrastructure). It was fun, and I could've easily seen it becoming a Geist game.<br /> <br /> That being said, what does your table want to play in? Obviously they're willing to ask for it, so talk to them about what aspects of the game they think would be engaging/fun, and ask them how they want to see that done. What is it that draws them to want to play Geist? Brainstorm with your players instead of alone, and it makes for a much better experience. (Not to mention easier.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>ST/DM for <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCfFy7kNgMvxIR1qdzT8PzEg\" target=\"_blank\">Dice Alternatives</a><br /> Playing: <a href=\"http://causality-and-consequences.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">DtD: Causality and Consequence</a><br /> Off-Season: <a href=\"http://lifelong-educations.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">M:tAw- Lifelong Education</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/667-abenniton","name":"ABenniton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar667_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-21T21:30:55-08:00","id":15866,"title":"","content":"Well their a several story hooks such as the fact that death happens all over world of darkness and any death the giest has to deal with the ghost so the story hook could involve who killed the ghost and how such as did a vampire kill him or a slasher concept or a better one is a ghost asking you to helping another supernatural for example a ghost died he needs to talk to his friend who is changeling or vampire or even scarier a slasher.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/893-reaperfrost8","name":"reaperfrost8","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar893_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-22T12:29:47-08:00","id":16229,"title":"","content":"A Lost Girl investigation game that focuses on the power struggles between two Krewes (the Light and the Dark), could also be interesting. Just replace every instance of a Fae with a Sin Eater.<br /> <br /> I am however, opposed to the idea of starting off with a supernatural antagonist right from the get-go, in any game (also I'm opposed to Justice League games, but that's personal). There is enough scary stuff within Geist (and also Book of the Dead) to leave you with plenty to run with (Rank 1 ghosts, Rank 3 ghosts, &quot;Unbound&quot; Geists, mortals, Chthonians, the Underworld, Kerberoi, rival Krewes, etc...). And when you finally run out (if ever), make up your own stuff. It keeps people guessing. More importantly, the antagonists from the other book? Most don't need context. Have your Sin Eaters deal with a Magath in Twilight. Or an Acamoth. Or a Hedgebeast. Cryptid. Stigmatic. Mortals. The ever popular zombies. Cursed swords. Blessed swords. You get my drift.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>ST/DM for <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCfFy7kNgMvxIR1qdzT8PzEg\" target=\"_blank\">Dice Alternatives</a><br /> Playing: <a href=\"http://causality-and-consequences.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">DtD: Causality and Consequence</a><br /> Off-Season: <a href=\"http://lifelong-educations.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">M:tAw- Lifelong Education</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/667-abenniton","name":"ABenniton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar667_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-22T13:07:10-08:00","id":16245,"title":"","content":"Idle suggestion to pick a city and look for things you can hook plot seeds onto.<br /> <br /> For instance, Washington D.C. has the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier and its Tomb Guard in nearby Arlington National Cemetery, and the annual National Cherry Blossom Festival makes for an interesting resonance with the notions of cultural exchange that the modern Bound see so much of \u2014 though the cherry trees are of different species, a little anachronism never stopped a good story from taking root; a krewe whose ethos includes the importance of truth and honesty and keyed off of the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parson_Weems#The_cherry-tree_anecdote\" target=\"_blank\">apocryphal story</a> of young George Washington's hatchet and English cherry tree might find something to talk about with a krewe that takes the imagery of the Japanese cherry tree and its <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherry_blossom\" target=\"_blank\">associated symbolism</a>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-22T14:17:26-08:00","id":16275,"title":"","content":"*eases onto someone's letter &quot;h&quot; with a low groan, rubbing his knee for a good measure or two....*<br /> <br /> The Cat Lady:<br /> <br /> Long, long ago, I once story-told in a multi-genre game; though primarily I had a sweet spot for the Geist-characters. (Their players were roleplaying exactly the way Geist-characters might behave; they -could- have helped ghosts. They -could- have had deathy-adventures. They -could- have made popcorn and watched movies; but they didn't. They kept running around with everyone else, and generally having a good old time. Because, as you'll note somewhere important in the book, that Sin Eaters aren't particularly compelled to be helpful in any direction, really; if only, perhaps by fear or guilt. (Or the promise for beer. Beer is a good motivator.) Oh, *wipes a tear from his eye, reminiscing*) I miss those old rascals....) Anyhow, I was/am infamous (among my direct peers) for creating plot-lines that poked you once or twice, then appeared to go quite dormant. (Which they never did.) One of them was The Cat Lady.<br /> <br /> Now, The Cat Lady in Life, was this sweet old woman, who only had two things in this bitterly-money-driven-and-game-deprived-world: her nephew, and her beloved cat, Spot. (A white short-hair, with a single, black spot on both sides.) Her nephew was really a money-grubbing-whore, and her cat was...well....*sighs longingly*....it was a silly-old-cat. The nephew murdered the cat out of spite, and put the poor damn thing in the old Lady's shed. And then, not too long thereafter, he strung her up in the attic. (She was a biter, but not really that strong: plus dentures are only good for what? Two or three REALLY-damn-awesome bites?) He did this with the hopes that he would inherit something tasty; or at the very least, green.<br /> <br /> But up until her murder, she was out on her front porch two or three times a day, calling out for her Spot to come home. (Because she missed him, gosh-Jeff-it!) Other cats from the neighborhood heard the sweet singing and calling of the lady; and THEY came. But it just made her miss her cat more; and she shoo'd those little bastards away.<br /> <br /> After she was mistletoe in the attic, her ghost descended back into the house proper, hardly aware or mindful of her death. She went back to her routine. Get up in the morning. Make coffee. Make toast with jelly, (that after she found she couldn't eat, she just threw away; she mustn't be hungry, after all!) and then watched her Soaps for the majority of the afternoon; now and then going outside and Calling for her Spot to return. As usual, other cats came to her, that did not belong; but she wanted Spot back so badly that it hurt. (Emotionally, of course: dear Old Cat Lady's body was ripe, but quite pain-free by this point.) She'd bring in those strays, or neighborhood cats, and cajole them over to Spot's favorite dish, and feed them. She was a sweet old bitty. And she was -convinced- that this time, her old eyes hadn't failed her: this was surely Spot. Because he was a good boy, and always came when Mummy called him.<br /> <br /> But soon, a part of her would Remember: that isn't Spot. That isn't Spot. That isn't Spot! THAT ISN'T MY $(*##@'n CAT!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!<br /> <br /> And after choking the life out of it, go bury the cat in her garden out back; after all, she didn't want the neighborhood children to see what she just did. That would be horrible. Then no one would come to her house on Halloween, trick or treating. (And she adored the children of her neighborhood.) After the daffy-dills were back in place, and the spade cleaned and placed back by the shed door, she would wander back inside her home; tired and depressed; sinking (quite literately) into her bed, and letting the darkness take her.<br /> <br /> The next morning, she would rise, and make herself some coffee, and toast with jelly, (that after she found she couldn't eat, she just threw away; she mustn't be hungry after all!) and then watched her Soaps for the majority of the afternoon; now and then going outside and Calling for her Spot to return.....<br /> <br /> And so forth. *grins, remembering.*<br /> <br /> Eventually, in my game, my players started noticing the number of flyers that were being posted on all the street poles about the family cat going missing; and a few rumors began circulating, (mostly in said neighborhood, and eventually the animal shelter) that there might be a budding sociopath; perhaps one of the younger children who was less social than the rest? Martin Rheeber was eventually taken to the psyche ward; eleven years old, and a loner. (That made a guilty few players start to really take notice.) I cannot recall the ridiculous reason I called forth to make it seem legit, but I assure you; it was at least half-way in that direction. Eventually, the PCs discovered the Cat Lady, figured her out, and helped her moved on. The Cat Dish was a momento mori that I decided was left behind; a twisted little piece of work that I reveled over frequently.<br /> <br /> Because, The Cat Dish does a minor, mind-effecting-shiney: You do a basic Composure+Resolve test. (One success works fine to negate effects) If you fail the first one, the character feels compelled to wash the Cat Dish. Some folks set their drying wares in a dish drainer. Some folks dry them off. The point is, they would touch it again. The next time they touched it, they had to do another simple test. (Same pool) If they failed again; they felt compelled to put cat food in the dish, and set it on their kitchen floor. (So yes, they'd drop everything, and go get a bag of catfood. Minor-effect again: they could resist with willpower to do other things; but eventually, they'd go get that bag of food.) After filling the dish, they'd get another test. A failure indicated that the Dish required a cat. They were compelled to get a cat. Cat-gotten (by whatever means, legal or otherwise) They let the cat eat from the dish. One last test. Failed, and they realized that this wasn't their cat. This wasn't their cat. THIS WASN'T THEIR EFFING CAT!!!!!!!!!<br /> <br /> And after choking the life out of it, they'd feel compelled (no test, just guilt from this point) to either bury the cat, or get rid of it in some fashion.<br /> <br /> Now, important thing: They'd have to pick The Cat Dish up to get rid of it. (&quot;You pick up The Cat Dish? Give me a composure+resolve simple test.&quot;) And you can see where this goes.<br /> <br /> Actually got to do that to one of my players. He got smart after the third (*Sadistic glee!!!!!*) time around and grabbed a towel to pick it up with.<br /> <br /> No, it's not a conventional Momento Mori, but each one has some crazy, death-related, small effect, and I likened this one to be a tad intense, and a touch scary. (Because Momento Mori should be!)<br /> <br /> <br /> *dusts off his &quot;h&quot;, after picking himself up, and toddles off to another thread....*","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1219-shake","name":"Shake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1219_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-24T02:40:54-08:00","id":17045,"title":"","content":"Pretty much every plot hook I get I work into my <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\">Actual Play Thread here</a>. Feel free to wade in and steal every idea you want to, or just get ideas from the posts. That's what it is there for, is for people to mine it for ideas for their own games. And keep up with the soap opera if they are so inclined.<br /> <br /> With Geist the PCs will often determine a lot about what the Krewe will be doing. I'd use that as a starting point. Another thing would be if you want linear adventures or Sandbox.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Mo